Ravi (AE): Heads-up — kickoff call with Marta (MediHealth) just finished. They want an on‑prem Private deployment (VPC/air-gapped option) for a 6‑month POC. Scope: streaming responses with function/tool calls to their EHR connector, and output strictly as structured JSON for downstream ingestion. Need: security questionnaire answers, quick SDK sample for streaming + function calls, and rough pricing for reserved capacity. @Sam can you own tech + sample?

Sam (SE): Yep, I can own the tech side. Quick clarifying Qs: 1) PHI — will they be sending real patient PHI? 2) target latency SLA? 3) concurrency/QPS expected for POC?

Marta (MediHealth): They will use de‑identified PHI during POC but eventual plan includes PHI. Latency target is sub‑300ms p50 for short prompts, concurrent streams ~20.

Liz (Solutions Eng): Noted. For on‑prem we'll need KMS/HSM integration + audit logging. Also confirm whether they require SOC2 + ISO attestation and an encrypted NFS for model artifacts. Marta, can you confirm compliance needs?

Marta (MediHealth): SOC2 required, ISO preferred. We have an internal KMS (Thales) and will require customer‑managed keys.

Chris (SecEng): I'll take security questionnaire. Action: I'll mark KMS/HSM as required and flag network isolation + minimalegress. Also need details on data residency and retention policy. Can you share their DPA expectations?

Ravi (AE): I'll loop legal for DPA. On pricing: want a 6‑month reserved estimate with burst capacity. Ballpark monthly for a 4x A10G equivalent pool + private control plane + enterprise SLA? Say ~USD 28k/mo — will confirm.

Sam (SE): I'll put together a tiny sample repo showing streaming + function calls that returns structured JSON. Minimal example below (JS streaming + tool call pattern):

```js
// pseudo SDK snippet for streaming + tool call -> structured JSON
const client = new RedwoodClient({ baseURL: 'https://private.redwood.local' })
const stream = await client.stream.chat.create({
  model: 'redwood-serve-structured-v1',
  messages: [{ role: 'user', content: 'Fetch medications for patient 12345' }],
  functions: [{
    name: 'ehr_lookup',
    description: 'Query EHR for patient data',
    parameters: { type: 'object', properties: { patient_id: { type: 'string' } }, required: ['patient_id'] }
  }],
  structured_output: { type: 'json', schema: { type: 'object', properties: { meds: { type: 'array' } }, required: ['meds'] } }
})

for await (const chunk of stream) {
  if (chunk.type === 'function_call') {
    const payload = JSON.parse(chunk.call_args)
    // call local adapter -> returns JSON string matching schema
    const result = await ehrAdapter.query(payload.patient_id)
    stream.respondFunctionResult('ehr_lookup', JSON.stringify(result))
  } else if (chunk.type === 'message') {
    process.stdout.write(chunk.delta)
  }
}
```

Sam (SE): I'll push that repo link in ~2 hours. Will include a Python variant and a tiny Dockerfile for the adapter.

Liz (Solutions Eng): For the structured output we should include a JSON Schema file and a strict validation step in the sample. That will help their engineers onboard quickly.

Ravi (AE): Cool. Marta — we'll send the security answers + sample repo + a formal POC quote by EOD tomorrow. Any blockers on your side for scheduling on‑prem install window?

Marta (MediHealth): We can target next Tuesday for initial install window. Need the install checklist (ports, services, expected disk).

Sam (SE): I'll attach the install checklist to the repo: ports 443, 50051 (gRPC), 8080 for admin API, 1.2TB disk for model cache (estimate), and systemd unit examples.

Chris (SecEng): Also note: customer will need an approved bastion host + IP allowlist for Redwoods support GT. We'll include a runbook and temporary cert exchange steps.

